ound you have some  intuition as to what your odds are of  hitting a jackpot the same thing on  every line or whatever that intuition  might be wrong  but at least you have some assessment of  that and when you map those digitally  all of a sudden the spaces between the  individual signs become slots or  possible landing zones I know that I'm  using the wrong language here but the  point is that that drastically skews the  odds not in the favor of the player and  the player may not even cognitively  realize that right there's all of these  interesting new layers of hidden  deception which just as an aside red  flag that they are all absolutely in  violation of existing consumer  protections but nobody's making any kind  of legal action against them because  there's just no no incentives in the  legal field to do that nobody  understands the computer technology etc  but you see this sort of sequential  layering of further levels of  obfuscation and deception and the one  that you're talking about would be when  mechanical real three mechanical reels  with 22 symbols each on them that used  to really limit the amount of a jackpot  you could give how often you could give  it and that's when you didn't really  want people hanging out for a long  period in front of the slot machines  once you started to be able to  manipulate that through what's called  the virtual real so you would still see  rolling in front of you these three  reels with 22 stops each and if you're  interacting with that over days and  weeks and sometimes even years for  regular gamblers you do get a kind of  intuitive sense even if you're not  conscious of it of what the odds are  because the odds are directly correlated  to the symbols that you are being shown  that are being presented to you on the  reels that link between perception what  you see and what the odds actually are  was completely severed by the  introduction of virtual reels because if  you imagine that inside the casino on  the chip 