 this  is very deceptive in terms of its  influence on the gambler isn't it  absolutely I mean if we're talking about  what we were just discussing these  virtual real and how it plays out on the  mechanical real  and it's using that mechanical real to  present the idea that you were very  close to winning when in fact you were  no closer to winning than if you had  been far away from that symbol so again  it's really manipulating the perception  of the user and that that really does  change user behavior so this near miss  effect carries over to things that even  came after the virtual real the virtual  real remember is still using three  actual mechanical reals since then you  know you still find those and they're  still popular and money drivers on the  casino floor but what's really risen to  the very top in terms of value to the  casino how much profit they reap are  what are called multi-line video slot  machines and you'll sometimes go into a  casino and you'll you'll look at these  machines you won't even recognize what's  going on typically it's a video screen  with a whole grid of many symbols that  are all moving as if they were reels  there could be ten reels with five lines  across and if you stand behind someone's  playing one of these you'll see that  they're not just betting two lineup  symbols straight across horizontally on  one line  there are many lines across there are  also diagonals  there are also zigzags and all sorts of  ways and sometimes there's just what's  called scatter which has no rationale at  all you can't really link anything up so  what people are doing is for the first  time on slot machines you're dividing up  your bet almost as if you're  diversifying your portfolio by investing  in many different machines at once it's  sort of like playing you know see if you  have a 300 line machine playing 300 slot  machines at once and you're betting  maybe a penny on each line so say you  know you're betting three dollars you're  going to probably win back on some of  those and when you do you're going to  hear ding ding ding ding it's going to  light up flash and you're going to hear  music ah you may win back three cents  out of your dollar  you're $3 you may win back 50 cents  that's a net loss in fact that the major  you've lost a major portion of your bet  but you're getting all the same  audiovisual reinforcement and brain  studies have shown when they hook people  up to scanners and other kinds of brain  metrics of measuring apparatus is that  your brain interprets this as an actual  win there's there's no distinction made  as you're rapidly cycling through these  things and for the casinos it ends up  being a way to extract money over time  while the player doesn't realize it's  happening they often refer to this as  giving the players a smoother ride so  it's like cushioning the effect it's  really interesting to me because it  seems to be against the whole sense of  gambling which is about volatility and  risk and suspense and sort of the thrill  of maybe losing maybe winning and this  is actually just sort of the same  reinforcement it lulls you into a kind  of trance  and whether you're winning whether  you're losing just ding ding dinging and  then it goes on and your eyes kind of  glaze over one designer told me that  these algorithms are like he said we  want to design a mathematical gaming  algorithm that's like reclining on a  comfortable couch and that was really  interesting to me when we talk about  gambling addiction as a problem and look  at some of the statements or assertions  that are made by the casino industry you  often hear for example you know only 1%  of the population really is susceptible  to or is you know has gambling addiction  and every time I read that I think to  myself right but a lot of those people  never go to casinos a lot of the 99%  that don't have this issue or not in  casinos and it's always seem more  relevant to me what percentage of the  people that gamble have a predisposition  or gambling addiction and and I'm  wondering whether we even have that data  right and so you know and also you have  to take out those under 21 etcetera so  if you if you look at a tennis  hard to find this data reliably in any  general sense you can find studies that  have done it that way in certain  locations etc but really on what you  want to look at is the percentage of the  adult gambling population that is and  even within that the percentage of the  adults gambling population that plays on  machines what percentage of them are  addicted I believe somewhere out there  is a statistic that the gaming industry  themselves came up with showing  something like 12% but their studies  shown suggesting that it's around 20%  and this really is in line with a lot of  other research showing that there's  something about the digital the solitude  the speed and the continuity of this  particular gaming format that makes it  uniquely addicting you know we always  like to talk about or I should say that  the gambling industry likes to talk  about genetic propensity to gamble as if  you know 1% of the population is born  attic and we need to worry about them  and how to mitigate their experience but  the rest of us can gamble with immunity  and really I think any addiction  researcher knows that to have an  addiction you can't just have a  predisposition you need an interaction  between a subject and an object and if  you look at the kind of interaction you  have with these slot machines it's far  more potent than say blackjack where  you've got to wait for your turn to come  again or horse racing where you've got  to wait for the race to finish there's a  term in addiction research called event  frequency and if you look at the event  frequency which literally means how many  times is happening you know within a  minute within an hour it's really off  the charts with these slot machines you  can play 1,200 hands an hour on those  new video slot machines that I was  telling you about and there's really  very little pause to self stop  to take to take stock of where you're at  to make a decision to keep playing or  not and that's why I feel that all of  this discourse around responsibility  it's kind of a moot point because it's  really difficult to have a responsible  consumer when the thing that they are  interacting with chips away at their  very capacity for responsibility through  this tight interaction solitude  continuity you can just keep going  there's never any moment to stop and the  sheer speed or event frequency and the  the gambling industry the casino  industry has sort of taken advantage of  the medicalization of gambling addiction  to sort of get get out from under at  least some of the responsibilities that  could be attributed to the mechanisms  and the machines right I mean it's very  easy to say hey listen we're talking  about a medical problem here we're just  an entertainment place most people don't  have this medical problem and thus we  don't really have to worry or be  scrutinized for how the machines work  but as you're talking about that's not  true because it ignores that a  predisposition does not necessarily mean  that you are going to start having a  problem it depends on the environment  and the machines and so many other  things exactly of course it's the case  that some people come in the world more  likely or more liable to become addicted  to things that present themselves along  the way but I always like to say that by  the same token certain objects and  substances come into this world far more  liable to addicts have the story when  you're talking about an addiction as an  interaction that builds between an  individual and an object or a substance  half the story lies with that object or  substance and that's really what my work  delves into I mean it's absolutely the  case that the industry prefers to look  only at the person side focusing not  just on genetics but on personal  biography and other kind of factors that  all point back to the individual because  that ends up deflecting attention away  from the products and onto the person  and you see this playing out  other domains as well it's very similar  to the guns don't kill people people  kill people  alcoholics don't come in bottles they  come in people I mean there's many  industries where you can find this kind  of rhetoric and the gambling industry is  no exception  where they are an exception is that they  have learned from the failures of say  tobacco to ignore at their own peril the  fact that this is that this addiction is  real and you can even find transcript of  industry summit online where they get  together and they decide you know in Las  Vegas we need to really craft the  rhetoric here we don't want to be like  those tobacco guys all raising our hands  later to be proven wrong and guilty and  lying saying you know addiction doesn't  exist there's nothing addictive in these  we want to be the guys who capture the  definition of what the addiction is and  how it's happening and by doing that  they throw money at genetic research etc  and they've even there's the gambling  industry themselves the American gaming  industry is the largest funder of  research around gambling addiction and  unsurprisingly despite claims to having  a you know a peer-review system etc that  would weed out by it unsurprisingly most  of it goes toward research which focuses  on the person part and you even see the  leaders of this National Center for  Responsible gaming that funds all this  research you see things on the homepage  like quote you know the slot machine  can't addictive it's just an object we  need to help the people so there's a  political discourse there's a PR  campaign behind this well the book is  absolutely fascinating we've been  speaking about Natasha Scholes book  addiction by design we could talk for  many more hours about it but  unfortunately that's all the time we  have but I highly recommend you check  out the book Natasha thanks so much for  talking to us about this and cribbing me  on David
 

